<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sri Vaishnavism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sri Vaishnava, Sri Vaishnava tradition</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sri <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnavism</a> is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> tradition devoted to the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> and his consort <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmi">Lakshmi</a>, or Sri, which grew from the Tamil devotional hymns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvars">Alvars</a>. It was systematised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramanuja">Ramanuja</a> in the 11th and 12th centuries, and its main centres include the temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangam">Srirangam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melkote">Melkote</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sri-vaishnavism.jpg" alt="Sri Vaishnavism" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ajay Goyal · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Srirangamlong_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
